WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
NASA will pay Boeing $359 million to return Starliner to flight and certify a new rocket. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 208. The funding will also help certify the Vulcan rocket for future missions in the 2030s. Boeing described itself as incredibly excited to serve as the nation's only astronaut transportation provider. A senior Boeing official joined a Starliner media call for the first time in two years. NASA plans

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:35.880
an un-piloted Starliner test flight at the end of the year. If successful, the agency and Boeing

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:42.640
hope to launch a piloted Starliner flight in mid-2028. The mission will incorporate redesigned

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:48.780
propulsion system valves and more robust subsystems. These modifications are expected to

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:55.240
prevent the failures that occurred in 2024. The next crude Boeing Starliner flight will not occur

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:56.500
until 2028.

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:02.060
Officials announced the timeline during a Monday update on commercial crew development plans.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:07.500
The schedule accounts for additional testing and system redesigns following earlier issues.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:13.660
While NASA shores up its commercial crew options, another partnership effort quietly widens

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:15.620
the circle of nations heading to the moon.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:18.900
Coat Divoir has signed the Artemis Accords.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:23.760
The signing increases the total number of signatories to 76 countries.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.420
African participation in the Accords continues to expand with this edition.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:35.680
Expanding partnerships on the ground sets the stage for deeper questions about how the very first galaxies assembled.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:43.460
Researchers have found direct evidence of neutral gas in galaxies that existed 700 million years after the Big Bang.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:49.900
The detection provides new insight into how the first stars and potentially planets formed and evolved.

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:54.660
involved. Scientists have worked to piece together events in the first billion years after

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:59.700
the Big Bang. Picture of volume of space the size of a large city block that contains

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:05.600
only a few dozen atoms of hydrogen spread across hundreds of light years. That sparse

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:10.320
material is what astronomers just detected directly in galaxies that formed when the

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:16.220
universe was less than 700 million years old. The gas sticks in vast reservoirs that have

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:21.940
not yet been turned into stars, so it remains invisible to optical telescopes, but reveals

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:26.800
itself through specific absorption lines in the light from even more distant quasars.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:32.260
Instruments on large ground-based telescopes captured those faint signatures by staring

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:37.620
at bright background objects for many hours and subtracting the foreground noise with extreme

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:38.140
precision.

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:43.660
The detection matters because neutral hydrogen is the raw fuel for the first generation

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:49.920
of stars and its presence at such early times shows that galaxy assembly was already well underway.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:55.540
Yet no one knows exactly how this gas cooled and collapsed fast enough to form stars

00:02:55.540 --> 00:03:01.260
before the universe reached 1 billion years of age. From the universe's first building blocks,

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:05.480
we shift to a much nearer test of new propulsion ideas built by students.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:11.780
Students in Cornell Space Systems Design Studio published results from two light-sale experiments.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:17.820
Alpha CubeSat and sailing to the stars were built by engineering students at Cornell University.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:21.780
The free-flying light sales were successfully deployed in orbit.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:29.780
The Cornell light-sale experiments demonstrate how student-built spacecraft can test propulsion concepts for future interstellar travel.

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:35.860
Two separate CubeSats deployed lightweight sales in orbit and collected data on their performance.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:42.240
These results show that small teams can contribute meaningful hardware to long-term exploration goals.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:49.020
The work opens practical paths for testing materials and control systems that larger missions could later scale.

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:55.180
Engineers at Cornell's Space Systems Design Studio published their findings after the flights concluded.

00:03:55.900 --> 00:04:02.740
Student hardware is already flying. NASA is now asking the wider community to help solve the next lunar challenge.

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:07.700
NASA issued the Human Lander Challenge to advance lunar landing technologies.

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:12.960
The challenge invites solutions for human lander systems supporting future Artemis missions.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:18.940
While challenges look ahead, one rover on Mars is once again sharing daily updates from

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:19.460
the surface.

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:24.160
The Perseverance Rover Updates page has resumed after a long hiatus.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:28.140
The rover has spent time exploring the Charms area on Mars.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:32.600
Regular updates will now continue with details on recent activities.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:39.020
On going Mars exploration pairs naturally with another crew preparing for a quick trip to low-earth orbit.

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:45.660
NASA's Kruth 13 mission could travel from Florida to the International Space Station in under nine hours.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:51.520
The crew will perform research, technology demonstrations, and maintenance aboard the orbiting laboratory.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:57.220
From crews heading up, we turned to new eyes looking down at potential asteroid threats.

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:03.040
NASA released a new documentary titled Planetary Defenders. This is not an exercise.

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:09.100
The film follows the story behind a recent real-world test of asteroid threat response procedures.

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:12.200
It is now streaming for free on NASA Plus.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:19.140
After looking at planetary risks, let's enjoy a striking celestial image and some seasonal stargazing tips.

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:25.640
Today's astronomy picture of the day shows SH2188 known as the Shrimp Nebula.

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:30.720
Each daily image includes a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:34.960
October's night sky notes focus on spooky stargazing themes.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:41.260
The ghoul star algal in Perseus represents the blinking eye of the Gorgon Medusa in Greek mythology.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:47.680
Viewers can spot the star along with Cassiopeia and Andromeda after 9pm during autumn months.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:52.920
Seasonal sky-watching leads us to a milestone in NASA's own flight test history.

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:58.220
NASA Armstrong celebrates 80 years of flight innovation on September 30th.

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:02.720
Five National Advisory Committee of Aeronautics engineers arrived at

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:09.080
Murak Army Airfield in 1946 to achieve supersonic flight. The X1 aircraft flew

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:13.900
faster than the speed of sound within two years. From Earth's atmosphere, we moved

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:18.820
to a spacecraft using it for a gravity assist on its way outward. The European

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:23.940
PN Space Agency's Jupiter IC Moons Explorer skimmed the outer edge of Earth's atmosphere

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:26.020
on September 28th.

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:29.720
The gravity assist altered its route to Jupiter using very little fuel.

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:34.680
The maneuver improves the spacecraft's course for its mission to the Jovian system.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:40.200
Finally, two quick notes on education and night sky viewing wrap up the episode.

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:44.500
Indigenous STEM students are set to participate in a NASA mission.

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:49.980
The program supports students in science, technology, engineering, and mathematics fields.

